thousands of public sector workers  protested in Paris they say they're  overworked and underpaid and they're  angry at the French government's plans  to reform the pension system and raise  the retirement age something they say  would erode their rights  I started working very young and I'm fed  up and very tired and all around us our  young people in their 20s who can find a  job so I should be able to retire so  they can work I can enjoy a few years of  my life while still in good health  we can barely survive we can't pay our  electricity bills at some point our  salaries have to increase  the French government says that it's  doing all that it can to Shield people  from inflation but it won't back down on  its pension reform plan that it says  would make the system fairer  we will also Implement a pension reform  we can do it in a fair way at a  reasonable Pace we can guarantee this  reform will be effective if we allow it  to finance our social protection system  there have been a number of smaller  and Strikes over the rising cost of  living in France over the past few  months but this one is by far the  largest with workers from across the  public sector and what trade unions says  unless their demands are met it will not  be the last and that is the point of the  strike of today it's not an end in  itself I hope it is going to be the  start of an upsurge in the coming winter  of strikes and resistance all across the  different workplaces in France  French president Emmanuel macron has  wanted to reform the pension System  since his first term but pushing it  through now is clearly a risk at a time  when so many people feel that their  lives are hard enough Natasha Butler Al  Jazeera Paris
 

